1
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:13,700
In the summer of 612 B.C.,
an army broke into Nineveh,

2
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:16,140
one of the greatest cities in the world,

3
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:18,440
and set its palaces on fire.

4
00:00:18,933 --> 00:00:22,160
Inside one of them was a library of clay.

5
00:00:22,749 --> 00:00:28,069
Tens of thousands of tablets,
gathered by one king who wanted copies of

6
00:00:28,109 --> 00:00:29,546
everything worth knowing.

7
00:00:29,896 --> 00:00:32,661
The fire was meant to destroy it.

8
00:00:33,011 --> 00:00:36,460
It is often said that the
fire saved it instead.

9
00:00:37,107 --> 00:00:42,260
The truth is stranger, and some of it was
only discovered in the last few years,

10
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:45,240
with the help of artificial intelligence.

11
00:00:45,877 --> 00:00:48,180
Not the legend, but the evidence.

12
00:00:53,332 --> 00:00:55,700
This is Ancient World Files.

13
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:03,260
Nineveh stood on the river Tigris,

14
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:08,960
in what is now northern Iraq, across
the river from the modern city of Mosul.

15
00:01:09,701 --> 00:01:14,780
In the seventh century B.C., it was
the capital of the Assyrian empire,

16
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:18,340
which stretched from the mountains
of Iran to the Mediterranean,

17
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:21,120
and for a time into Egypt.

18
00:01:21,691 --> 00:01:26,120
Its kings guarded their palaces
with stone giants like this one:

19
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:28,520
winged bulls with human heads.

20
00:01:29,093 --> 00:01:34,000
In 669 B.C., a new king
came to the throne.

21
00:01:34,582 --> 00:01:39,340
His name was Ashurbanipal, and he
was a younger son who had not been

22
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:40,740
expected to rule.

23
00:01:41,244 --> 00:01:44,380
Because of that, he had
been trained as a scholar.

24
00:01:44,964 --> 00:01:50,260
In his own inscriptions he boasted that
he could read difficult tablets in ancient

25
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:55,140
languages, even inscriptions on
stones from before the Flood.

26
00:01:55,723 --> 00:01:59,780
Few kings of his age ever
claimed they could read at all.

27
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:02,520
This one wanted to know everything.

28
00:02:03,122 --> 00:02:06,460
And he ruled an empire
that took what it wanted.

29
00:02:12,045 --> 00:02:14,680
Writing in Assyria meant clay.

30
00:02:15,272 --> 00:02:19,860
A scribe pressed the corner of a
cut reed into a soft clay tablet,

31
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:22,240
leaving tiny wedge-shaped marks.

32
00:02:22,742 --> 00:02:27,420
Ashurbanipal ordered copies of
every important text in his kingdom,

33
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:29,840
and his scribes worked for years.

34
00:02:30,455 --> 00:02:35,880
Today, more than thirty thousand tablets
and fragments from Nineveh survive.

35
00:02:36,472 --> 00:02:38,860
And the library also held writing boards:

36
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:42,900
hinged panels of ivory
or wood, covered in wax,

37
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:45,080
that have almost all perished.

38
00:02:45,689 --> 00:02:48,380
But this was not a library of stories.

39
00:02:48,985 --> 00:02:53,440
Most of it was practical: how to read
the future in the liver of a sheep,

40
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:58,120
in the movements of the stars,
in the birth of a deformed lamb.

41
00:02:58,723 --> 00:03:01,960
There were spells against
demons, medical treatments,

42
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:04,760
and long lists of words in two languages.

43
00:03:05,533 --> 00:03:08,300
It was a working library
for the king's experts,

44
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:12,540
whose job was to warn him
of danger before it came.

45
00:03:13,109 --> 00:03:15,620
Many tablets end with a note of ownership:

46
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:19,320
palace of Ashurbanipal, king of the world,

47
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:20,700
king of Assyria.

48
00:03:21,204 --> 00:03:24,800
Some add a curse on anyone
who steals the tablet.

49
00:03:25,349 --> 00:03:29,180
That is an irony, because many
of the tablets were taken.

50
00:03:29,782 --> 00:03:32,020
After a civil war with his own brother,

51
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:37,880
who ruled Babylon, Ashurbanipal's
officials gathered tablets from the old

52
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,820
temple cities of the south.

53
00:03:40,410 --> 00:03:45,740
One surviving record lists about two
thousand tablets and three hundred writing

54
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:48,380
boards brought into
the collection at once,

55
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,680
many from private libraries in Babylonia.

56
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:57,281
The greatest library of its age
was built partly by confiscation.

57
00:04:05,631 --> 00:04:09,860
Ashurbanipal died around 631 B.C.,

58
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:13,340
and within twenty years
his empire was gone.

59
00:04:13,853 --> 00:04:17,420
His successors fought each other,
and Babylon broke free.

60
00:04:18,027 --> 00:04:21,980
A Babylonian general named
Nabopolassar made himself king,

61
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:25,060
and the Medes, from the mountains of Iran,

62
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:27,760
rose under their king Cyaxares.

63
00:04:31,528 --> 00:04:36,820
In 614 B.C., the Medes
captured and sacked Assur,

64
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:38,900
the ancient religious capital.

65
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:43,240
Two years later, the Medes and
the Babylonians came together

66
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:45,320
against Nineveh itself.

67
00:04:45,943 --> 00:04:50,980
A clay tablet known as the Babylonian
Chronicle records what happened.

68
00:04:51,572 --> 00:04:53,720
The siege lasted about three months,

69
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:57,340
through the summer of 612 B.C.

70
00:04:58,131 --> 00:05:00,360
Then the attackers broke into the city,

71
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:02,580
plundered the palaces and temples,

72
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:07,260
and, in the Chronicle's words,
turned the city into a ruin heap.

73
00:05:07,782 --> 00:05:11,300
Archaeologists have found the
evidence of that fighting.

74
00:05:11,864 --> 00:05:16,680
At one of the city gates, excavators
uncovered the skeletons of people killed

75
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:20,600
in the gateway, with arrowheads
lying among the bones.

76
00:05:24,292 --> 00:05:25,820
The palaces burned.

77
00:05:26,462 --> 00:05:31,220
Their roofs and walls collapsed onto
the rooms where the tablets were kept.

78
00:05:31,731 --> 00:05:35,000
And this is where the
famous story comes from.

79
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:37,960
Most tablets were only dried in the sun,

80
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:42,740
so the story says the fire baked
them hard and preserved them forever.

81
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:46,400
There is some truth in it:
heat can harden clay,

82
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:48,240
and some tablets were fired.

83
00:05:48,774 --> 00:05:51,340
But the fire also wrecked the library.

84
00:05:51,946 --> 00:05:55,460
Shelves and floors collapsed,
tablets were smashed,

85
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:59,580
and most of what survives today is
not whole tablets but fragments.

86
00:06:00,253 --> 00:06:04,800
The fire did not save the library
so much as bury it in pieces.

87
00:06:13,470 --> 00:06:17,960
For more than two thousand years,
the ruins lay under great mounds of earth

88
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,440
across the Tigris from Mosul.

89
00:06:21,195 --> 00:06:27,020
In the 1840s, European
explorers began digging into

90
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:29,880
them, looking for the cities of the Bible.

91
00:06:30,494 --> 00:06:36,120
In 1849, a young Englishman named
Austen Henry Layard tunnelled into

92
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:38,300
the mound called Kuyunjik.

93
00:06:38,831 --> 00:06:41,320
He found the palace of Sennacherib,

94
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:47,620
Ashurbanipal's grandfather, with dozens
of rooms lined with carved stone panels.

95
00:06:48,139 --> 00:06:50,400
The reliefs showed sieges, battles,

96
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:53,680
and kings, carved in astonishing detail.

97
00:06:54,205 --> 00:06:56,780
And in two of the rooms, Layard wrote,

98
00:06:57,100 --> 00:07:02,020
the floor was covered with broken clay
tablets to a depth of a foot or more.

99
00:07:02,509 --> 00:07:07,120
Then, in 1853, Layard's
assistant Hormuzd Rassam,

100
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:10,900
who had been born in Mosul,
made a second discovery.

101
00:07:11,439 --> 00:07:15,800
The northern part of the mound had
been promised to French excavators,

102
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:19,600
so Rassam's workmen dug
there secretly, at night.

103
00:07:20,151 --> 00:07:23,460
They found the palace
of Ashurbanipal himself,

104
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:26,220
with its famous lion hunt reliefs,

105
00:07:26,260 --> 00:07:28,140
and thousands more tablets.

106
00:07:28,748 --> 00:07:31,040
The finds were shipped
to the British Museum,

107
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:34,700
and Victorian Britain
went mad for Assyria.

108
00:07:35,207 --> 00:07:37,940
But almost no one could read the tablets.

109
00:07:38,622 --> 00:07:41,200
Cuneiform had only just been deciphered,

110
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:47,180
and in 1857 four scholars were each
sent a copy of the same new inscription

111
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:49,740
to translate on their own.

112
00:07:50,224 --> 00:07:52,720
Their sealed translations matched closely,

113
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:55,780
and the script was accepted as readable.

114
00:08:04,448 --> 00:08:08,940
Among the people who came to look at the
tablets was a young banknote engraver

115
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:10,540
named George Smith.

116
00:08:11,142 --> 00:08:15,460
He had left school at fourteen, but he
spent his lunch breaks in the museum,

117
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:18,420
teaching himself to read cuneiform.

118
00:08:19,060 --> 00:08:22,240
He was so good at it that
the museum gave him a job,

119
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:24,940
sorting the fragments from Nineveh.

120
00:08:25,445 --> 00:08:31,200
In 1872, Smith picked up a broken
tablet and read about a ship that

121
00:08:31,240 --> 00:08:35,960
came to rest on a mountain,
and a bird sent out to look for dry land.

122
00:08:36,449 --> 00:08:40,140
It was a flood story,
told by a man named Utnapishtim,

123
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:45,440
who survived a great flood sent by
the gods by building a huge boat.

124
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:48,560
According to an account
written years later,

125
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:52,020
Smith jumped up, rushed
around the room in excitement,

126
00:08:52,060 --> 00:08:54,600
and began to take off his clothes.

127
00:08:55,305 --> 00:09:00,220
On the third of December, 1872,
he presented the tablet in London,

128
00:09:00,260 --> 00:09:03,240
and the Prime Minister, William Gladstone,

129
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:04,920
came to hear him.

130
00:09:05,313 --> 00:09:09,760
The tablet was part of what we
now call the Epic of Gilgamesh,

131
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,620
one of the oldest great
works of literature.

132
00:09:13,196 --> 00:09:17,580
And its flood story had roots
far older than Ashurbanipal:

133
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:23,380
Babylonian versions were already
being copied around 1700 B.C.

134
00:09:24,195 --> 00:09:26,980
A London newspaper, the Daily Telegraph,

135
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:31,340
paid for Smith to go to Nineveh
and look for the missing pieces.

136
00:09:31,899 --> 00:09:34,820
Astonishingly, within
days of starting to dig,

137
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:39,280
he found a fragment that seemed
to fill a gap in the flood story.

138
00:09:39,815 --> 00:09:44,540
But later scholars showed it belonged to
a different version of the flood story,

139
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:46,440
not to the same tablet.

140
00:09:49,986 --> 00:09:53,440
George Smith died of illness
on his third expedition,

141
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:56,520
near Aleppo, in 1876.

142
00:09:57,161 --> 00:09:59,324
He was thirty six years old.

143
00:10:07,674 --> 00:10:11,920
In June 2014, the Islamic
State group seized Mosul.

144
00:10:12,762 --> 00:10:18,580
In February 2015, it released a video
of its fighters smashing statues in the

145
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:23,020
Mosul Museum and drilling into
the face of a winged bull at

146
00:10:23,060 --> 00:10:25,140
Nineveh's Nergal Gate.

147
00:10:25,701 --> 00:10:30,140
Weeks later, it blew up the
palace of Ashurnasirpal at Nimrud,

148
00:10:30,340 --> 00:10:33,140
the palace this winged bull came from.

149
00:10:33,612 --> 00:10:38,740
In 2016, two of Nineveh's gates,
partly rebuilt in the twentieth

150
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:40,240
century, were bulldozed.

151
00:10:40,674 --> 00:10:44,280
The people who did this
called it a war on idolatry.

152
00:10:44,769 --> 00:10:49,620
But some of it was also looting,
and it left something unexpected behind.

153
00:10:50,269 --> 00:10:53,160
After the city was retaken in 2017,

154
00:10:53,780 --> 00:10:58,080
Iraqi archaeologists entered tunnels
the militants had dug beneath

155
00:10:58,120 --> 00:10:59,700
a destroyed shrine.

156
00:11:00,344 --> 00:11:03,160
Inside, they found walls, sculptures,

157
00:11:03,540 --> 00:11:06,540
and inscriptions of a
palace of Esarhaddon,

158
00:11:06,580 --> 00:11:11,500
Ashurbanipal's father, that no one
had seen for twenty six centuries.

159
00:11:11,999 --> 00:11:15,960
Destruction and discovery,
again, in the same place.

160
00:11:24,407 --> 00:11:29,980
Back in the museums, the library of
Ashurbanipal is still being put together.

161
00:11:30,474 --> 00:11:32,760
The fire left tens of
thousands of fragments,

162
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:35,140
and for a hundred and fifty years,

163
00:11:35,180 --> 00:11:38,740
scholars matched them by eye, one by one.

164
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:44,460
In 2023, a team at the University
of Munich made public a database

165
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:50,207
called the Fragmentarium, with
photographs and texts of more than twenty

166
00:11:50,247 --> 00:11:51,700
two thousand fragments.

167
00:11:52,351 --> 00:11:57,780
An algorithm compares the fragments and
suggests which ones belong together.

168
00:11:58,327 --> 00:12:01,820
It has already found new lines
of the Epic of Gilgamesh,

169
00:12:01,860 --> 00:12:06,400
and pieces of a hymn to the city
of Babylon that had been lost for

170
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:07,860
two thousand years.

171
00:12:08,418 --> 00:12:12,720
And thousands of fragments are
still waiting to be identified.

172
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:18,760
So, did the fire save the library?

173
00:12:19,330 --> 00:12:20,360
Not really.

174
00:12:20,982 --> 00:12:24,540
What saved it was clay,
which outlasts paper and parchment,

175
00:12:24,580 --> 00:12:28,800
and burial, which hid it from
looters for twenty five centuries.

176
00:12:29,472 --> 00:12:32,400
And people: an excavator who dug at night,

177
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:36,440
a banknote engraver who taught
himself an extinct script,

178
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:40,120
and the researchers who are still
fitting the pieces together.

179
00:12:40,651 --> 00:12:44,180
The library was built by a
king who took what he wanted,

180
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:47,740
and it was destroyed by
armies who took everything.

181
00:12:51,885 --> 00:12:53,920
But almost everything we know about it,

182
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:57,260
we learned only in the
last two hundred years.

183
00:12:57,831 --> 00:13:02,840
Somewhere in a museum drawer, there is
still a fragment of clay with a line of

184
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:05,400
Gilgamesh on it that no one has read.

185
00:13:05,897 --> 00:13:09,140
The library burned two and
a half thousand years ago,

186
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:10,959
and we are still reading it.

187
00:13:18,309 --> 00:13:21,120
Thank you for watching
Ancient World Files.

